{"type":"user","message":{"role":"user","content":[{"tool_use_id":"toolu_01LYczgBGFEsLQbeutfEQqCc","type":"tool_result","content":"Web search results for query: \"O binding energy Cu(111) fcc hollow DFT atomic oxygen eV Xu Mavrikakis\"\n\nLinks: [{\"title\":\"Quantum Many-Body Simulations of Catalytic Metal Surfaces\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.13036\"},{\"title\":\"Embedded quantum computing for many-body surface reaction\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.27009\"},{\"title\":\"Accurate absolute core-electron binding energies of molecules, solids and surfaces from first-principles calculations\",\"url\":\"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.04823\"},{\"title\":\"unibo.it\",\"url\":\"https://acnpsearch.tweb-dev.unibo.it/singlejournalindex/86725655\"},{\"title\":\"arxiv.org\",\"url\":\"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914\"},{\"title\":\"Calculating binding energy shifts in core level XPS from first principles: a case study of adsorbates on Cu(111)\",\"url\":\"https://meetings-archive.aps.org/mar/2018/s01/10\"},{\"title\":\"PhysRevB 73 165424\",\"url\":\"https://condensed.physics.usyd.edu.au/old_website/papers_aloy/PhysRevB_73_165424.pdf\"},{\"title\":\"Adsorption and dissociation of O2 on Cu(111):: thermochemistry, reaction barrier and the effect of strain\",\"url\":\"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/867115130\"},{\"title\":\"peeref.com\",\"url\":\"https://peeref.com/works/68773180\"},{\"title\":\"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface\",\"url\":\"https://https-meetings--archive-aps-org-443.webvpn1.xju.edu.cn/mar/2022/g47/5/\"}]\n\nThe search turned up the Xu and Mavrikakis result, though only in abstract fragments, not the full paper. Their 2001 *Surface Science* study reports that atomic oxygen on Cu(111) favors threefold hollow sites, with a binding energy of roughly -4.3 eV relative to a gas-phase O atom (Atomic oxygen is found to prefer threefold hollows, with a binding energy of ca. -4.3 eV with respect to a gas-phase O atom.). The same paper's study of strain is also relevant: expansive strain parallel to the surface plane is shown to enhance the binding of atomic and molecular oxygen.\n\nOther DFT work on this system uses different conventions and gives different numbers. One study reports fcc and hcp O binding energies of -5.000 eV and -4.905 eV, and states that threefold hollow sites, both fcc and hcp, are the energetically preferred binding sites for O* with a binding energy of -5 eV and -4.905 eV, respectively. A different study defines binding energy with the opposite sign, so positive values indicate stable adsorption (a positive number indicates that the adsorption is exothermic). The gap between the -4.3 eV and -5.0 eV values likely reflects differences in functional, slab model, and reference state, but I could not confirm this from the sources retrieved.\n\n\nREMINDER: You MUST include the sources above in your response to the user using markdown hyperlinks."}]},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"24387e54-a126-4a5c-9040-a5860d1c47ed","uuid":"6fb5fbec-dc27-4365-ac0b-37cd11739993","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:54:48.538Z","tool_use_result":{"query":"O binding energy Cu(111) fcc hollow DFT atomic oxygen eV Xu Mavrikakis","results":[{"tool_use_id":"srvtoolu_013Fqh3HJF1X7HXfeMPteXNh","content":[{"title":"Quantum Many-Body Simulations of Catalytic Metal Surfaces","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2508.13036"},{"title":"Embedded quantum computing for many-body surface reaction","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/2607.27009"},{"title":"Accurate absolute core-electron binding energies of molecules, solids and surfaces from first-principles calculations","url":"https://arxiv.org/pdf/1904.04823"},{"title":"unibo.it","url":"https://acnpsearch.tweb-dev.unibo.it/singlejournalindex/86725655"},{"title":"arxiv.org","url":"https://www.arxiv.org/pdf/2505.12914"},{"title":"Calculating binding energy shifts in core level XPS from first principles: a case study of adsorbates on Cu(111)","url":"https://meetings-archive.aps.org/mar/2018/s01/10"},{"title":"PhysRevB 73 165424","url":"https://condensed.physics.usyd.edu.au/old_website/papers_aloy/PhysRevB_73_165424.pdf"},{"title":"Adsorption and dissociation of O2 on Cu(111):: thermochemistry, reaction barrier and the effect of strain","url":"https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/867115130"},{"title":"peeref.com","url":"https://peeref.com/works/68773180"},{"title":"First principles study of oxidation resistance of atomically flat Cu(111) surface","url":"https://https-meetings--archive-aps-org-443.webvpn1.xju.edu.cn/mar/2022/g47/5/"}]},"The search turned up the Xu and Mavrikakis result, though only in abstract fragments, not the full paper. Their 2001 *Surface Science* study reports that atomic oxygen on Cu(111) favors threefold hollow sites, with a binding energy of roughly -4.3 eV relative to a gas-phase O atom (Atomic oxygen is found to prefer threefold hollows, with a binding energy of ca. -4.3 eV with respect to a gas-phase O atom.). The same paper's study of strain is also relevant: expansive strain parallel to the surface plane is shown to enhance the binding of atomic and molecular oxygen.\n\nOther DFT work on this system uses different conventions and gives different numbers. One study reports fcc and hcp O binding energies of -5.000 eV and -4.905 eV, and states that threefold hollow sites, both fcc and hcp, are the energetically preferred binding sites for O* with a binding energy of -5 eV and -4.905 eV, respectively. A different study defines binding energy with the opposite sign, so positive values indicate stable adsorption (a positive number indicates that the adsorption is exothermic). The gap between the -4.3 eV and -5.0 eV values likely reflects differences in functional, slab model, and reference state, but I could not confirm this from the sources retrieved."],"durationSeconds":8.512533236000017,"searchCount":1},"tool_result_meta":[{"id":"toolu_01LYczgBGFEsLQbeutfEQqCc","permission_decision":