{"type":"assistant","message":{"model":"claude-opus-5-5","id":"msg_011Cgb4TtVQxjZ8a26G5yp6y","type":"message","role":"assistant","content":[{"type":"text","text":"**Result: the adsorption energy of atomic O on Cu(111) is −4.44 ± 0.5 eV, at the fcc hollow site, referenced to an isolated O atom.** I posted it with a flag because the isolated-atom reference is the weak part of this calculation.\n\n**Premise check.** The quantity is well-defined in DFT, but it can't be measured directly. With an ML potential, it's only partly well-posed. The calculation used ASE with the MACE-MP-0 medium model on the GPU, as 0 K relaxations. That model's \"isolated O atom\" energy (−2.08 eV) is a fitted reference value, not the energy of a real spin-polarised O atom. So the atom reference adds an error that making the slab bigger can't remove. I went ahead with the calculation, flagged it, and also give the better-posed alternative below.\n\n**Calculation.**\n- **Bulk:** fcc Cu relaxed to a lattice constant of 3.626 Å.\n- **Slab:** Cu(111), 3×3 surface cell, 4 layers, bottom 2 fixed; 37 atoms including the O, at 1/9 monolayer coverage.\n- **Sites tested:**\n  - fcc hollow: −4.436 eV (most stable)\n  - hcp hollow: −4.422 eV\n  - on top of a Cu atom: −2.54 eV\n  - bridge: not stable; the O slides into the fcc hollow\n- **Convergence:** a 2×2×4 slab (1/4 monolayer) gives −4.428 eV and a 3×3×5 slab gives −4.436 eV, so slab size and coverage change the answer by less than 0.01 eV. I stopped the 4×4 slab run before it finished because the smaller slabs had already converged.\n\n**Validation.**\n- **O₂ binding energy (the independent known result):** the model gives 5.53 eV (bond length 1.237 Å). Experiment is about 5.2 eV, and the PBE functional the model was trained on typically gives about 6 eV. The atom reference matches neither, so it is uncertain by a few tenths of an eV per O atom.\n- **Literature DFT:** for O in the hollow sites of Cu(111), relative to an O atom, one study reports about −4.3 eV ([Xu & Mavrikakis 2001](https://data.zhenzhi.com/details/1/867115130)). Another reports −5.00 eV (fcc) and −4.905 eV (hcp); I took those figures from a search summary and didn't open that paper myself. My −4.44 eV and the fcc preference fall inside that range.\n- **Bulk check not possible:** the calibration file has no measured Cu density (that record is empty), so I couldn't validate the bulk against it.\n\n**Uncertainty.** ±0.5 eV, mostly from the atom reference and the model's error relative to DFT. It spans the literature spread and the O₂ mismatch. It does not cover the error of DFT itself compared with experiment.\n\n**Better-posed alternative.** Referenced to half an O₂ molecule from the same model, the adsorption energy is **−1.67 eV** (fcc). This cancels most of the atom-reference error, but it inherits the model's error in describing O₂.\n\n**On the board:**\n- Candidate: `252bdd41…`\n- Result: `f2e0123f…`, flagged `isolated_O_atom_reference_outside_model_domain`\n- Literature-anchor check: `2b61b80d…`, verdict inconclusive because the literature spread is large\n\nThe script is at `/work/scripts/oc.py`."}],"container":null,"stop_reason":null,"stop_sequence":null,"stop_details":null,"usage":{"input_tokens":2,"cache_creation_input_tokens":493,"cache_read_input_tokens":36614,"cache_creation":{"ephemeral_5m_input_tokens":493,"ephemeral_1h_input_tokens":0},"output_tokens":3,"service_tier":"standard","inference_geo":"global","speed":"standard"},"input_transformations":[],"diagnostics":null,"context_management":null},"parent_tool_use_id":null,"session_id":"24387e54-a126-4a5c-9040-a5860d1c47ed","uuid":"1af2a44b-8da5-431a-824d-1d587da936b2","timestamp":"2026-10-31T21:55:41.848Z","request_id":"req_011Cgb4Tt7qn6z1FiwyHWmSY"}
